NOTES = {
1: "This chapter follows one real payment from the moment a shop shows an invoice until the receiving wallet can spend the coin. Alice pays Bob's Store, and every slide adds one piece of the mechanism: the invoice, the transaction, the choice of coins, the signing, the block and the rules every node checks. Fourteen code examples run under Python 3.14.6, and each result is computed by the chapter library, never typed by hand. The deck follows chapter 2 of Mastering Bitcoin, third edition, by Antonopoulos and Harding, and each slide names its sources.",
2: "The chapter is divided into six parts that follow one payment from invoice to spending, and the route on this slide lists the slide range of each part. The first part introduces the questions and the chapter map. The next parts cover the store and its invoice, the transaction itself, the choice of coins, the construction of the payment, its journey into a block and finally the rules that govern spending. Three true stories and fourteen code examples are spread across these parts, and the closing slides collect the summary and the resources.",
3: "These six questions summarise the chapter, and each one is answered on a slide that is named in the summary. They ask what a payment and an invoice contain, why inputs must equal outputs plus a fee, how a wallet chooses coins, how a payment reaches a miner, why confirmations make a payment harder to reverse, and which rules every node checks. They concern mechanisms and reasons, not vocabulary. A reader who can answer all six in their own words has understood the path of one payment through the Bitcoin network.",
4: "One payment passes through six steps: the invoice, the transaction, the coins, the construction, the block and the spending. The invoice tells Alice what to pay. The transaction states inputs, outputs and a fee. Coin selection picks the outputs to spend and returns the surplus as change. Construction prices and signs the payment. A miner then places it in a block, and each further block adds a confirmation. Finally every node checks the rules when the receiver spends the new coin. Coins never travel; only records of ownership change.",
5: "Part 2 begins where Alice does, with an invoice from an online store. Three cards preview what follows: the system as a whole, the satoshi as the smallest unit and the payment link that carries the invoice. The whole system consists of users, wallets, nodes and miners who share one record that nobody owns. A satoshi is one hundred-millionth of a bitcoin, so every amount inside the network is a whole number. The cards are a preview, and the slides after this one develop each of them with an example.",
6: "Four roles share the work of a payment. A wallet holds keys and signs payments for its user. Nodes check every rule and pass valid transactions on to their peers. Miners bundle waiting transactions into the next block. The blockchain is the shared record of blocks that every node keeps. Nobody owns this record, so no single party can change it alone. A wallet does not store coins; it stores the keys that prove the right to spend outputs recorded on the blockchain.",
7: "In June 2010 almost nobody could buy bitcoin, because no exchange yet served ordinary people. Gavin Andresen, a software developer in Massachusetts, announced on 11 June 2010 a website that gave five bitcoins to every visitor who solved a CAPTCHA. He had stocked it with 1,100 of his own coins, enough for 220 visitors, and served requests in order of arrival. Thousands of people received their first bitcoin this way. Alice needs coins before she can pay, and this true story shows how early users obtained theirs. The source thread on bitcointalk is linked on the slide.",
8: "Bitcoin counts in satoshis, and one satoshi is one hundred-millionth of a bitcoin. The invoice amount of 0.01577764 bitcoin becomes the whole number 1,577,764, and converting back returns exactly the same text. Whole numbers are used because decimal fractions held in floating point lose digits, which is unacceptable for money. The table shows how one bitcoin, the invoice amount and a small payment of 75,000 satoshis are written as whole satoshis. The chapter library function converts in both directions, and the result card shows the amount written two ways.",
9: "A payment link follows the format defined in BIP 21, which wallets and QR codes both use. The code splits the link into the part before the question mark and the query that follows it. The scheme is the word bitcoin, which tells the phone to open a wallet. The query holds the amount and the label, and the address sits before the question mark. The table lists the four parts that the wallet reads to fill in the payment. Query text is URL-encoded, so the apostrophe in the shop name appears as the characters %27.",
10: "Bob's link is one string made of five parts: the scheme, the address of the store, the amount, a separator and the label. The scheme bitcoin followed by a colon tells the phone which application should open. A BIP 21 link carries the address, the amount and the label together, so nobody types an address. A QR code on the checkout page holds exactly this text, and a camera reads it. The code is not encrypted. A BIP is a numbered public proposal, because nobody owns Bitcoin and changes begin as open design documents.",
11: "Part 3 opens the payment itself. A transaction spends earlier outputs through its inputs and creates new outputs that lock amounts to new owners. Each input points back to an earlier output, so a chain of transactions leads back to where the coins began. When a spent coin is larger than the payment, the surplus returns to the payer as a new output called change. The three cards preview inputs and outputs, transaction chains and change, and the following slides give the exact figures for Alice's payment.",
12: "A transaction is double-entry bookkeeping: inputs are listed on the left, outputs on the right, and the difference between them is the fee. Figure 2-2 of Mastering Bitcoin shows this layout. An input is a reference to an earlier output that is being spent, together with a signature that proves the right to spend it. An output is an amount of bitcoin locked to a new owner until that owner spends it. An input does not contain money; it only points to the output that does.",
13: "The picture draws Alice's payment to scale. One input of 100,000 satoshis is spent, and the outputs are 75,000 to Bob's Store and 20,000 returned to Alice as change. The remaining 5,000 satoshis are claimed by no output, so the miner who includes the transaction collects them as the fee. Both bars have the same length because inputs equal outputs plus fee. The fee is not a field of the transaction; it is only what is left over. The four cards define the double-entry ledger, the input, the output and the fee.",
14: "Alice's transaction has one input of 100,000 satoshis and two outputs that total 95,000 satoshis. Subtracting the outputs from the inputs gives a fee of 5,000 satoshis. The chart shows the three amounts side by side, and the three slices add up to the input, so the ledger balances. Nowhere in the transaction is the fee written down, since it is implied by the difference between the two sums. A reader who looks for a fee field will not find one, because the miner simply collects whatever the outputs leave unclaimed.",
15: "Inside a transaction the identifier of the earlier transaction is stored in one byte order, and block explorers display the reverse order. The code reads the 32 stored bytes and reverses them. The first eight hex digits are eb3ae38f in the stored form and 4ac54180 in the displayed form. The table shows both orders of the same identifier. Because two tools show different text for the same value, pasting the stored form into an explorer finds nothing. The correct habit is to reverse the bytes before comparing identifiers.",
16: "Three names meet on this slide. An unspent output is one that no later input has consumed, and every full node keeps the set of them. An outpoint names exactly one output by combining a transaction identifier with an output number, written with a colon between them. An input never holds coins; it only names an earlier output through its outpoint. The identifier appears in two byte orders, the displayed one and the stored one, which are the same 32 bytes read in opposite directions.",
17: "A miner ranks transactions by fee per virtual byte, not by the total fee. Alice's transaction weighs 569 weight units, which is 143 virtual bytes after dividing by four and rounding up. The 5,000 satoshi fee divided by 143 virtual bytes gives a rate of about 34.97 satoshis per virtual byte. Dividing the fee by the weight, or by the plain byte count, would give a wrong rate. The miner's block space is limited, so the rate shows how much each unit of space pays.",
18: "Size is measured in weight units. A plain byte counts four units, while a witness byte, which holds signature data, counts one unit. Alice's transaction has 125 plain bytes and 69 witness bytes, so its weight is 125 times four plus 69 times one, which is 569 weight units. Dividing by four gives its size in virtual bytes. Signature bytes are discounted so that signatures weigh less than the data that creates ownership. Miners rank transactions by the fee paid for each virtual byte, not for each plain byte.",
19: "Figure 2-3 shows three linked transactions. Each input names an earlier output, such as Tx1:0, and following these arrows backwards leads to the transaction where a coin began. A spent output can never be spent a second time, and this single rule is the whole defence against double spending. Every full node can verify the rule because it holds the complete chain of transactions. Reading the figure from right to left shows the history of a coin, and reading it from left to right shows how ownership moved.",
20: "Collecting every output that some input has used leaves the outputs that no input has used, and those are the unspent outputs. The code builds the set of used outputs, the table of values and the list of free outputs. The result lists Tx1:1, Tx2:0 and Tx3:0 as the coins that exist in the chain of figure 2-3. A wallet balance is the sum of unspent outputs that the wallet can sign for. Spent and unspent describe the state of an output, which is a different matter from how old it is.",
21: "A script locks an output, and the input that spends it supplies the key. In the picture the output of Tx2 demands Bob's signature, and Bob's payment provides it, so the script evaluates to true and the spend is allowed. Serialization then turns the whole transaction into bytes made of fixed fields: version, inputs, outputs, witness and lock time. Bitcoin scripts are small on purpose and are not a general programming language. A script can demand more than one signature, but it never loops.",
22: "Part 4 asks how a wallet chooses what to spend. Coins are spent whole, so the choice of inputs decides how much change comes back. Three common transaction forms are shown. A payment sends value to another person. A consolidation merges many small inputs into one output. Batching pays several people in one transaction. All three are shapes of inputs and outputs that follow the same ledger rule, so inputs minus outputs is always the fee.",
23: "A wallet cannot cut a coin in half, so it takes whole coins until the payment is covered and returns the surplus as change. With coins of 60,000, 30,000 and 25,000 satoshis and a need of 80,000, the library picks 60,000 and 30,000 and returns 10,000 as change. The chart shows the picked coins and the change bar. Without a change output the whole surplus would go to the miner as a fee. Real wallets use more careful rules than this one, which weigh fees and privacy.",
24: "The diagram reads from left to right. Grey outputs have been spent, and teal outputs are coins that still exist. The output of 20,000 satoshis is Alice's change, an ordinary output sent to a new address that she controls. The choice of coins affects both fees and privacy: spending many inputs makes a larger transaction that costs more and reveals that these coins share an owner. A wallet therefore balances the size of the transaction against the amount of change it creates.",
25: "Payment, consolidation and batching are three shapes of the same ledger rule. The code counts inputs and outputs for each shape and computes its fee as inputs minus outputs. The results are 5,000 satoshis for the payment, 1,500 for the consolidation and 3,000 for the batch. A business that pays many salaries would use batching, which shares one transaction among many receivers. Consolidation merges coins of one owner and should not be confused with mixing coins of different owners, which it does not do.",
26: "The same rule holds whether one coin or many are spent: inputs minus outputs is the fee. The picture draws the three shapes with their amounts in satoshis. In the payment, 100,000 satoshis become 75,000 for Bob, 20,000 change and a 5,000 fee. In the consolidation, several small inputs become one output with a small fee. In the batch, one input pays several receivers at once. Batching saves the most per receiver, and consolidation is best done when fees are low.",
27: "Part 5 builds Alice's transaction in four tasks: find the inputs, write the outputs, leave a fee and sign. The wallet looks for unspent outputs that together cover the payment and the fee. It then writes down who receives how much and where the change goes. The fee rate chosen by the wallet decides how much change comes back. Signing comes last, because a signature covers the finished transaction. The next slides show each task with numbers.",
28: "The wallet builds the transaction in five steps. It finds inputs that cover the payment and the fee, writes the outputs for the receiver and the change, computes the fee as the difference, signs each input with the private key and finally serializes the result. The wallet decides only the inputs, the outputs and the fee, and the rules do the rest. Only the signing step needs the private key. A wallet does not send coins; it sends a signed record that other nodes can check.",
29: "The shop quotes a dollar price, and the wallet turns it into a payment in six steps. It divides the price by the exchange rate at checkout, which gives 0.01577764 bitcoin, then multiplies by 100,000,000 to obtain whole satoshis. The wallet pairs the amount with the address from the invoice and creates an output that Alice can sign for. The invoice must be paid soon because the exchange rate changes. The wallet never works with decimals and holds keys, not coins.",
30: "The fee is the fee rate multiplied by the size of the transaction, and the change is what remains of the input after the payment and the fee. With a size of 143 virtual bytes, rates of 10, 20, 35 and 100 satoshis per byte leave change of 23,570, 22,140, 19,995 and 10,700 satoshis. The bars fall as the rate rises. A fee is not a percentage of the payment; it depends on the size of the transaction. A payer in no hurry can choose a low rate and wait longer for a block.",
31: "A hash is a fingerprint of data. The two texts differ by one digit, yet their fingerprints share nothing recognisable, and the hash cannot be turned back into the text. A key pair holds a private key, which is a 256-bit secret, and a public key, which is a point on a curve computed from it. Computing the public key from the private key is easy, and the reverse is infeasible. This one-way property separates checking, which anyone can do, from signing, which only the key holder can do.",
32: "Signing and checking use the key pair in opposite directions. The wallet signs the message with the private key, and any node checks the message and the signature with the public key. The signature proves approval and nothing is hidden: every transaction is public. A checksum protects an address against typing slips, because one changed character makes it fail. Signing is therefore different from encrypting, which would hide the message. In Bitcoin nothing is hidden; the signature only shows who approved.",
33: "Part 6 follows the signed transaction into a block. Miners search for a number that makes a block of transactions valid, and each valid block on top of Alice's makes her payment harder to reverse. A confirmation is a block that includes the payment or builds on it. Before any block the payment waits unconfirmed in the memory pools of nodes. The slides that follow describe the network, the spread of the payment, the structure of a block and the search that miners perform.",
34: "The network has no server. Every participant is a peer, which means another node that keeps a connection and exchanges transactions and blocks as an equal. A full node downloads every block and checks every rule itself, so it trusts nobody but needs storage and bandwidth. A lightweight client keeps keys but fetches only headers and proofs, so it is small and must trust the nodes that serve it. The peer-to-peer network is formed by all of these links between equals.",
35: "A payment spreads from Alice's wallet to a few nodes and then to many. Each node checks the payment against the rules before it forwards the payment to its peers, so invalid transactions stop at the first honest node. This spreading is called gossip, and it needs no coordinator. The route does not matter, because the payment reaches every node and the miners among them. Because the spread is decentralised, it is fast and hard to stop.",
36: "Transactions that nodes have accepted wait in the memory pool, which is sorted by fee rate. The bracket marks the transactions that fit in the next block. Alice's transaction pays 34.97 satoshis per virtual byte, so it sits between higher-paying and lower-paying neighbours. Bob can see the payment in a block explorer, which is only a window onto the pool and the chain of one server. The pool of one node can differ from the pool of another node, because each node receives transactions at slightly different times.",
37: "A miner assembles a candidate block from waiting transactions. The block is a header of 80 bytes followed by a list of transactions, with the coinbase transaction first. The header has six fields: the version of 4 bytes, the previous block hash of 32 bytes, the merkle root of 32 bytes, the time of 4 bytes, the target of 4 bytes and the nonce of 4 bytes. The miner hashes only the header. A candidate block becomes a block only when its header hash is below the target, which is proof of work.",
38: "Alice's transaction sits in block 277,316. Block height counts the position of a block from the genesis block, and block depth counts the blocks on top of it. A block has one confirmation when it contains the payment, and each further block adds one more. Figure 2-7 shows the chain with the heights marked. Height and depth are different numbers that answer different questions, so the confirmations of a payment are counted from its depth, not from its height.",
39: "The ruler runs from the genesis block to Alice's block and beyond it. Before any block contains the payment, it waits in the memory pool with zero confirmations. When block 277,316 contains it, the payment has one confirmation, and the next blocks raise the count to two, three and more. After block 277,318 the payment has three confirmations. Each added block makes the payment harder to reverse, because reversing it would mean rebuilding every block on top.",
40: "A miner changes the nonce and hashes again until the hash starts with enough zeros. The code searches for a number that gives a hash starting with one, two, three and four zeros, and it needs 33, 302, 10,182 and 32,977 tries. Each added zero costs about sixteen times more tries, which the log-scale bars show. The result is luck, not skill, and no shortcut exists. Checking the answer takes one hash, so anyone can verify the work quickly.",
41: "This slide gives the small ideas under every hash. A bit is one binary digit, and eight bits make a byte, so a 256-bit hash is 32 bytes. The byte 0xEB is 1110 1011 in bits, EB in hexadecimal and 235 in decimal. Entropy measures how unpredictable a number is, which sets the size of the search. The nonce table shows that changing one number changes the whole hash, and that 33 is the first nonce whose hash starts with a zero.",
42: "The genesis block was mined on 3 January 2009. Its header packs six fields into 80 bytes, and the name of the block is the hash of this header. The code hashes the header and prints the first twenty digits of the result, which start with ten zeros: 000000000019d6689c08. Bitcoin hashes the header twice and then reverses the bytes for display, a step that is easy to forget. The zeros show that the work needed to find this block was large.",
43: "A merkle tree builds one root from many transactions. Pairs of transaction hashes are hashed together, level by level, until a single root remains, so the root commits to every transaction in the block. Proof of work requires the block hash to lie below a target. Difficulty sets this target, and it changes every 2,016 blocks, not at every block. The hash rate is the total number of hashes per second that miners perform, and a higher rate leads to a higher difficulty.",
44: "Two blocks can arrive at the same height, which is a fork. Nodes follow the chain with the most accumulated work, called the best chain, and the next block decides which branch wins. The transactions of the losing block return to the memory pool, unless the winning chain already contains them. A double spend fails because only one of two conflicting transactions can be in the best chain. This is why waiting for confirmations protects a receiver.",
45: "On 31 October 2008 an author writing as Satoshi Nakamoto posted a nine-page paper to the metzdowd.com cryptography mailing list. Section 11 computes the attacker's odds, modelling the race between the honest chain and a secret one as a random walk. Its table gives the chance that an attacker with a share of the hash power catches up after a number of confirmations. The paper is available at bitcoin.org, as the link on the slide shows. Satoshi Nakamoto is a pseudonym, and the next slide reruns the table.",
46: "Rerunning the formula of section 11 reproduces the paper's table to seven decimal places. The code computes the catch-up probability for an attacker with 10 percent of the hash power and for one with 30 percent, for zero to six confirmations. After one confirmation the first attacker has a chance of 0.2045873, and after six it is 0.0002428. Six confirmations became a habit for this reason. A larger attacker never makes the odds vanish; the odds only become smaller.",
47: "The chance that an attacker catches up falls very fast with each confirmation, so waiting longer makes a reversal much less likely. The table reads downwards, one row for each confirmation. A shop may accept a small payment with no confirmation, because waiting costs more than the risk of losing it. A large payment deserves several confirmations. The number of confirmations to wait for is therefore an economic choice, based on the cost of losing the payment, and not a technical rule.",
48: "Part 7 closes the loop. Bob spends his output, and every node checks the same consensus rules before it accepts the spending transaction. A block that breaks a rule is refused by every node, and its miner earns nothing. A block explorer is a website where anyone can look up blocks, transactions and addresses. The slides that follow show one rule, the halving of the reward, and one incident in which a missing rule let a block create 184 billion bitcoin.",
49: "The block reward is a consensus rule: every node computes it in the same way and refuses a block that claims more. The reward starts at 50 bitcoin and is halved every 210,000 blocks, which gives 50, 25, 12.5, 6.25, 3.125 and 1.5625 bitcoin in the first six eras. The code shifts the amount of 50 bitcoin right by the number of eras. The halving is a rule that every node enforces, and not a promise about the price.",
50: "The first transaction of every block is the coinbase. It has no input, only the coinbase text, and its one output pays the reward plus the fees to the miner. This creates the coins that pay for mining. Finding a block is hard, yet checking it is quick. Many small miners join a pool and share one reward in proportion to their shares. The slide shows the coinbase and the pool.",
51: "On 15 August 2010 block 74,638 contained a transaction that paid out about 184 billion bitcoin, thousands of times the limit of 21 million. Two of its outputs were so large that their sum wrapped around in 64-bit arithmetic, and the check that outputs do not exceed inputs passed. Jeff Garzik spotted the block within the hour, and Satoshi Nakamoto shipped version 0.3.10 about five hours after the block. The honest chain overtook the bad one by block 74,691, and the forged coins ceased to exist.",
52: "Two outputs of 9,223,372,036,854,277,039 satoshis each were added as 64-bit signed numbers. The sum wrapped around to the negative number -997,538, so the old check that outputs do not exceed inputs passed. The code reproduces this step with the chapter library. No cryptography was broken and no key was stolen; a rule was missing. One new rule, a limit on each output and on their sum, ended the problem, and nodes that adopted it rejected the bad block.",
53: "Rules are checks that every full node makes: proof of work below the target, no output above its inputs, a coinbase not above the reward and a block within the size limit. A rule change is safe when old nodes still accept the new blocks. A soft fork shrinks the set of valid blocks, so old nodes keep following the chain. A hard fork enlarges the set, so nodes that stay behind split from the chain. A hard fork is therefore more than a large upgrade.",
54: "Spending onward extends the chain: Bob's output becomes the input of his own payment. A wallet that does not hold the whole chain can still check that a payment is in a block, using a short proof. The merkle path needs only a handful of hashes, three in the picture, to link the transaction to the root in the header. This is simplified payment verification. The proof shows that the payment is included in the block; it does not show that the payment is wise.",
55: "The chain can be written as a graph of statements, each made of a subject, a predicate and an object, called a triple. In the provenance vocabulary, outputs are entities, transactions are activities and key holders are agents. The graph links output Tx2:1 to the transaction Tx2 that created it, and to the output Tx1:0 that Tx2 used. A graph can answer questions about origin that a plain list cannot. An ontology is a model of the field, and it is not a database.",
56: "Five demonstration pages let readers try the mechanism. Each page adds one idea to the one before it: the hash, the block, the chain, the peers and the coinbase. Predicting what breaks when an early block is edited is a good exercise, because the edit changes its hash and every link after it. The pages are interactive, so a few minutes with each teach the mechanism more directly than rereading the slide. The ladder on the slide shows the order of the pages.",
57: "Each row answers one of the six opening questions in a line. A payment contains inputs, outputs and a fee, and the fee is what the outputs leave unclaimed. A wallet chooses whole coins and returns the surplus as change. A miner collects the payment and searches for a valid block. Each confirmation makes a reversal less likely. Every node checks the rules, and a block that breaks one is refused. Covering the right column and answering from memory is a good test.",
58: "These links are open and free to use, and every one was opened on 25 October 2026. The chapter source and the developer guide give the full detail of inputs, outputs and fees, and the white paper holds the catch-up table in section 11. A block explorer lets a reader look up a real transaction today. An explorer is a reading tool: a reader should never enter a private key or a seed phrase into it or into any website.",
59: "This is the whole path of one payment, from the invoice to a spendable coin. The questions of slide 3 are answered in the summary, and every result in the deck was computed by the chapter library under Python 3.14.6, not typed by hand. Chapter 3 opens Bitcoin Core, the reference implementation, whose software checks every rule described here. Questions on any of the six answers are welcome.",
}
